2048 is Gabriele Cirulli's game.
<abstract>
Help for 2089, the game of 2048 for the desktop.
<\metainfo>

<hometopic>2089 Help

2089 is the game of 2048 for the desktop.  Slide the tiles on the
board; when two tiles with the same number meet, they join into one
worth both.  Join your way up to a 2048 tile to win, and keep going if
you like, until no move is left.

<list bullet>
* <link HowToPlay>How to Play<\link>
* <link Keys>Keys and Buttons<\link>
* <link Menus>Menus<\link>
* <link Colours>Colours<\link>
* <link SavedGames>Saved Games<\link>
* <link Starting>Starting 2089 from a Command<\link>
<\list>


<s1 id=HowToPlay>How to Play

The game starts with two tiles on a board of four rows of four.  Each
move slides every tile as far as it will go in one direction: up,
down, left or right.  Two tiles with the same number that meet join
into one, with their sum on it, and your score goes up by that sum.
After every move that changes the board, a new tile appears, a 2 or
(one time in ten) a 4.

You win when a tile reaches 2048.  You can play on after that, for as
long as some move can still change the board.  When none can, the game
is over.

The best score for each size of board is kept, and shown in the Best
box.  Scores are kept in the file .2089 in your home folder, which the
2089 programs for terminals share.


<s1 id=Keys>Keys and Buttons

Click the board, then type:

<list bullet>
* The arrow keys, or <computer>w a s d<\computer>, or
<computer>k h j l<\computer>, or <computer>8 4 2 6<\computer> on the
keypad: move.
* <computer>u<\computer>: undo the last move, if undo is on.
* <computer>n<\computer>: start a new game.
* <computer>p<\computer>: show the save string.
* <computer>c<\computer>: the next colour scheme.
* <computer>?<\computer>: the keys.
* <computer>q<\computer>: exit.
<\list>

Or play with the mouse: click the arrow buttons beside the board to
move, and New Game and Undo below them.


<s1 id=Menus>Menus

<list bullet>
* File: New Game, Open a saved game, Save As a file, show the Save
String, and Exit.
* Edit: Undo the last move.  Undo is on when 2089 is started from the
desktop, or from a command with <computer>-u<\computer>.
* Options: the colours of the board.  See <link Colours>Colours<\link>.
* Help: this help, the keys, help on using help, and about 2089.
<\list>


<s1 id=Colours>Colours

The Options menu has three colour schemes for the board.  The rest of
the window always takes the desktop's colours.

<list bullet>
* Game Colours: the colours of the original 2048, from beige to
orange to gold.
* Desktop Colours: the colours of your desktop palette.  Choose
another palette in Style Manager, and the next time 2089 starts, its
board will follow.
* Black and White: tiles from outlined to reverse video as they grow,
for monochrome displays.
<\list>


<s1 id=SavedGames>Saved Games

Choose Save As from the File menu to keep a game in a file.  Its name
should end in <computer>.2089<\computer>.  Choose Open to play it
again, or double-click it in File Manager.

A saved game is one line of text, its save string.  Save String in the
File menu shows the string of the game being played, to copy into a
note or a mail message.  Paste one in and choose Play to play it.

When you log out, the game being played is saved with your session,
and comes back when you log in again.


<s1 id=Starting>Starting 2089 from a Command

Type <computer>xm2089<\computer> in a terminal, with any of these:

<list bullet>
* <computer>-u<\computer>: allow undo.
* <computer>-s<\computer> <emph>size<\emph>: a board from 2 to 8
squares across.
* <computer>-r<\computer> <emph>seed<\emph>: the same seed starts the
same game.
* <computer>-l<\computer> <emph>savestring<\emph>: play a saved game.
* <computer>-colours<\computer> <computer>game<\computer>,
<computer>desktop<\computer> or <computer>mono<\computer>: the colour
scheme.
* A file name: open a saved game.
<\list>

When you exit, 2089 prints the command that carries on the game.

There are 2089 programs for terminals, too: <computer>2089<\computer>,
which plays full screen or a line at a time on any terminal, and
<computer>x2089<\computer>, which needs nothing but the X Window System.
